# =============================================================================
# Email notifications via AWS SES (Simple Email Service)
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS DOES:
#   When the monitor catches a 4xx / 5xx failure, or when a flow fails, or
#   when you click "Snapshot & report" — the backend sends an email via AWS
#   SES to whoever you listed in that project's Settings → Notification
#   emails. (Slack still works the same way via the webhook URL on the project.)
#
# IF YOU SKIP THIS FILE ENTIRELY:
#   The app still runs. Checks keep happening, the dashboard works, Slack
#   still fires. The only thing that breaks is email — every send returns
#   { sent: false, reason: "SES not configured (...)" } silently. That's fine
#   for a dev box. Configure it only when you actually want emails.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 1 — Copy this file to .env
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   In a terminal, from the backend/ folder:
#
#       cp .env.example .env             (Mac/Linux)
#       copy .env.example .env           (Windows cmd)
#       Copy-Item .env.example .env      (Windows PowerShell)
#
#   .env is gitignored — your real credentials will NEVER be committed.
#   Never edit .env.example with real values; that file IS committed.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 2 — Verify a "From" identity in AWS SES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   You can ONLY send mail from an address (or domain) that SES has verified.
#
#     1. Sign in to AWS Console → Simple Email Service (SES) → Verified identities
#     2. "Create identity" → pick "Email address" (fastest) or "Domain"
#          - Email   : you'll get a click-to-verify link in that inbox. Done.
#          - Domain  : add the 3 CNAME DKIM records SES generates to your DNS.
#                      Recommended for production — lets you send from ANY
#                      address @yourdomain.com.
#     3. The address you verified is what you put in SES_FROM_EMAIL below.
#
#   NEW ACCOUNTS LAND IN SES SANDBOX — important:
#     In sandbox, you can ONLY send TO addresses that are ALSO verified.
#     For real use, request production access from the SES console
#     (one-page form, usually approved in a few hours). After that, send-to
#     anyone is allowed.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 3 — Decide HOW the backend authenticates to AWS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   The AWS SDK auto-resolves credentials from (in order):
#
#     OPTION A — Env vars (simplest, what most devs use locally)
#     ----------------------------------------------------------
#       Put AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID + AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY in .env below.
#       The IAM user / role those keys belong to needs ses:SendEmail policy
#       (and ses:SendRawEmail for the audit attachment path).
#
#     OPTION B — ~/.aws/credentials file (also fine for local dev)
#     ------------------------------------------------------------
#       If you've run `aws configure` previously, the SDK picks up your
#       default profile automatically. Use AWS_PROFILE=<name> to override.
#       Leave AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY UNSET in .env then.
#
#     OPTION C — IAM role (production: EC2 / ECS / Lambda / EKS)
#     ----------------------------------------------------------
#       NO credentials in env at all — the SDK reads instance metadata.
#       This is the recommended production setup; key rotation handled by AWS.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 4 — Fill in the values below (in your .env, NOT this file)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Required:
#     AWS_REGION         — the SES region where you verified the identity,
#                          e.g. us-east-1, eu-west-1, ap-south-1.
#                          MUST match the identity's region — sending from a
#                          different region won't find it.
#     SES_FROM_EMAIL     — the verified sender. Can be a bare address
#                          (monitor@example.com) or a full From header
#                          ('Monitoring Suite <monitor@example.com>').
#
#   Required only when using OPTION A above:
#     AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
#     AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
#     AWS_SESSION_TOKEN  — only needed if your creds are temporary (SSO / STS).
#
#   Optional:
#     SES_CONFIGURATION_SET — name of an SES configuration set to attach to
#                             every send. Use this if you want SES to emit
#                             open/click/bounce/complaint events to a SNS
#                             topic, CloudWatch, or Kinesis Firehose.
#                             Leave blank if you don't need engagement events.
#     AWS_PROFILE           — only if using OPTION B with a non-default
#                             profile from ~/.aws/credentials.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 5 — Restart the backend so it picks up the new env
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   The backend reads AWS_* + SES_* once at startup. After editing .env:
#
#       (Ctrl+C the running `npm run dev`, then start it again)
#
#   tsx watch auto-reloads on .ts file changes but NOT on .env changes.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 6 — Tell each project who should receive emails
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   In the UI: open a project → click the ⚙ cog → Notifications panel →
#   either "General failure emails" or "Latency failure emails" → paste one
#   or more addresses (comma, semicolon, or newline separated). Save.
#
#   While in SES sandbox, remember: every TO address must also be verified
#   in SES. Add each recipient as a verified identity OR request production
#   access first.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 7 — Verify it works
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Quickest test: in the UI, open any project that has Notification emails
#   set, click "📊 Snapshot & report". You should receive an email within
#   a few seconds with the HTML report attached. The Audit result modal
#   will also show email: { sent: true } for confirmation.
#
#   If email: { sent: false, reason: "..." } — check the reason:
#     "SES not configured (set AWS_REGION + SES_FROM_EMAIL ...)"
#                                          → those two env vars aren't both set
#     "Email address is not verified..."   → sandbox + recipient not verified
#                                            (verify them OR request prod access)
#     "The security token included in the request is invalid"
#                                          → AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / SECRET wrong
#     "Could not load credentials from any providers"
#                                          → SDK couldn't find creds anywhere
#                                            (env / ~/.aws / IAM role)
#     "Region is missing"                  → AWS_REGION not set
#     "no recipients"                      → project has no Notification emails set
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY NOTES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   • NEVER paste real AWS keys into THIS file (.env.example is committed).
#   • .env IS gitignored — safe for real credentials. Double-check with:
#         git check-ignore -v backend/.env
#     (should print the .gitignore line that matches).
#   • Production: prefer IAM roles over long-lived access keys. Rotate keys
#     at least every 90 days when they're unavoidable.
#   • Grant the LEAST privilege the backend needs:
#         {
#           "Version": "2012-10-17",
#           "Statement": [
#             {
#               "Effect": "Allow",
#               "Action": ["ses:SendEmail", "ses:SendRawEmail"],
#               "Resource": "*"
#             }
#           ]
#         }
#     Don't give the monitor IAM admin or ses:* — these two actions are
#     all it ever calls.
#   • SES has a 40 MB raw-message size limit. The audit HTML report is
#     usually a few hundred KB — well under. If it ever exceeds, the send
#     will fail with "Message size exceeds limit" and the rest of the run
#     keeps going.
#
# =============================================================================

AWS_REGION=us-east-1
SES_FROM_EMAIL=Monitoring Suite <monitor@example.com>

# OPTION A — env-var credentials (simplest local-dev path).
# Comment these out if you're using OPTION B (~/.aws/credentials) or
# OPTION C (IAM role on EC2/ECS/Lambda).
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=AKIA_REPLACE_WITH_YOURS
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=replace_with_your_secret
# AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=only_needed_for_sso_or_temporary_creds

# Optional — attach an SES configuration set for engagement events.
# SES_CONFIGURATION_SET=monitoring-events
